# `i < N`, and N is a variable of the module that the loop's function reads: the read is written in the head of the loop, so that as an input value it lies in the loop, though no turn writes the variable.
# It counts as made in front of the loop: there the variable is read once, freed of its dead zone and rounded, and the copy behind the tests goes by the lesser of the bound and the greatest int32: its counter is an int32.
# Without that the counter may come to 2^53, where a step no longer moves it: every step was made on doubles and taken back into an integer.
# const N = Number(process.argv[2]);
# function sum() { let s = 0; for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) s += i & 7; return s; }
# (globalThis as any).kept = sum;
# console.log(sum());
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = GetModuleVariable variable=g0
# CHECK: [[rounded]]: int @Int64 = IntegerBound
# CHECK: = TypeTest [[rounded]] testedType=i32
# CHECK: frequency=Rare isLoopHead=true
# CHECK: = Increment {{*}}form=CheckedIntegers
# CHECK: int[0, 2147483647] @Int32) isInLoop=true isLoopHead=true
# CHECK: = Less {{*}}form=Integers
# CHECK: @Int32 = Increment {{*}}form=Integers

